export const notes=[
 ['Know the consumer','Study what people choose, use, and experience in context. Observe behavior and ask about the circumstances behind it rather than relying on demographic labels alone.'],
 ['Cultural, social & personal','Investigate shared traditions, influence from other people, and the individual’s life circumstances. A family celebration can involve all three.'],
 ['Groups & family','Find out who recommends, pays, decides, and uses. A person can also be attracted to a community they hope to join.'],
 ['Motivation','Ask what outcome the consumer is pursuing and how strongly it matters now. The same product can serve different motives for different buyers.'],
 ['Maslow as a study lens','Remember physiological, safety, belonging, esteem, and self-actualization needs. Use examples to distinguish them; avoid treating the hierarchy as an inflexible prediction.'],
 ['Perception','Separate noticing a message from interpreting it. A relevant message can win attention yet still be misunderstood through prior beliefs or ambiguous wording.'],
 ['Emotion & memory','Consider a remembered birthday, general knowledge about a brand, and knowing how to order. These illustrate episodic, semantic, and procedural memory respectively.'],
 ['Encoding & retrieval','A new association must first be formed and later brought to mind. A useful cue at the moment of choice may help a consumer remember an earlier message.'],
 ['The buying journey','Follow the buyer from recognizing a need through searching, comparing, purchasing, and using. Familiar purchases can involve much less active deliberation.'],
 ['Evaluate the choice','Compare a weighted score with a strict cutoff or a rule based on the most important attribute. State the rule before predicting the choice.'],
 ['Involvement & risk','Look for the consequence the buyer fears: poor performance, injury, lost money, wasted time, others’ judgment, or conflict with their own self-image. Match assistance to the expressed concern.'],
 ['After purchase','Investigate both what was promised and what was experienced. Provide useful onboarding and support; do not assume a completed transaction ends the customer’s evaluation.']
];
